      "text": "The colony starts with 3 bacteria, and doubles every day. This means that the number of bacteria at the end of day \\(n\\) can be represented by the formula \\(N(n) = 3 \\cdot 2^{n-1}\\), where \\(N(n)\\) is the number of bacteria at the end of day \\(n\\).\n\nWe need to find the smallest \\(n\\) such that \\(N(n) > 100\\). That is, we need to solve the inequality \\(3 \\cdot 2^{n-1} > 100\\).\n\nFirst, we divide both sides of the inequality by 3:\n\\[2^{n-1} > \\frac{100}{3}.\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\(\\frac{100}{3}\\):\n\\[\\frac{100}{3} \\approx 33.33.\\]\n\nSo, we need to find the smallest integer \\(n-1\\) such that \\(2^{n-1} > 33.33\\). We can check the powers of 2:\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n2^1 & = 2, \\\\\n2^2 & = 4, \\\\\n2^3 & = 8, \\\\\n2^4 & = 16, \\\\\n2^5 & = 32, \\\\\n2^6 & = 64.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nWe see that \\(2^5 = 32\\) is less than 33.33, and \\(2^6 = 64\\) is greater than 33.33. Therefore, \\(n-1 = 6\\), so \\(n = 7\\).\n\nThe number of the first day which ends with the colony having more than 100 bacteria is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).",
