      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the number of the first day on which the colony of bacteria has more than 100 bacteria, we need to analyze the pattern of growth. The colony starts with 3 bacteria and doubles every day. We can express the number of bacteria at the end of each day as follows:\n\n- At the end of day 0 (initial state): \\( B(0) = 3 \\)\n- At the end of day 1: \\( B(1) = 2 \\times B(0) = 2 \\times 3 = 6 \\)\n- At the end of day 2: \\( B(2) = 2 \\times B(1) = 2 \\times 6 = 12 \\)\n- At the end of day 3: \\( B(3) = 2 \\times B(2) = 2 \\times 12 = 24 \\)\n- At the end of day 4: \\( B(4) = 2 \\times B(3) = 2 \\times 24 = 48 \\)\n- At the end of day 5: \\( B(5) = 2 \\times B(4) = 2 \\times 48 = 96 \\)\n- At the end of day 6: \\( B(6) = 2 \\times B(5) = 2 \\times 96 = 192 \\)\n\nWe can see that the number of bacteria at the end of day \\( n \\) is given by \\( B(n) = 3 \\times 2^n \\). We need to find the smallest \\( n \\) such that \\( B(n) > 100 \\).\n\n\\[\n3 \\times 2^n > 100\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by 3:\n\n\\[\n2^n > \\frac{100}{3} \\approx 33.33\n\\]\n\nThe smallest integer \\( n \\) that satisfies this inequality is 6, because \\( 2^6 = 64 \\) and \\( 2^5 = 32 \\). Let's verify:\n\n\\[\n2^5 = 32 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2^6 = 64\n\\]\n\nSince \\( 64 > 33.33 \\), the smallest \\( n \\) that satisfies the inequality is indeed 6. Therefore, the number of the first day which ends with the colony having more than 100 bacteria is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
