      "text": "To determine the number of the first day which ends with the colony having more than 100 bacteria, we start by understanding the pattern of growth. The colony starts with 3 bacteria and doubles every day. We need to find the smallest integer \\( n \\) such that the number of bacteria at the end of day \\( n \\) is greater than 100.\n\nThe number of bacteria at the end of day \\( n \\) can be expressed as:\n\\[ 3 \\times 2^n \\]\nWe need to find the smallest \\( n \\) such that:\n\\[ 3 \\times 2^n > 100 \\]\n\nFirst, we solve the inequality:\n\\[ 2^n > \\frac{100}{3} \\]\n\\[ 2^n > \\frac{100}{3} \\approx 33.33 \\]\n\nNext, we find the smallest integer \\( n \\) that satisfies this inequality. We can do this by testing successive values of \\( n \\):\n- For \\( n = 5 \\):\n  \\[ 2^5 = 32 \\]\n  Since 32 is less than 33.33, \\( n = 5 \\) is not enough.\n- For \\( n = 6 \\):\n  \\[ 2^6 = 64 \\]\n  Since 64 is greater than 33.33, \\( n = 6 \\) is enough.\n\nTherefore, the number of the first day which ends with the colony having more than 100 bacteria is:\n\\[ \\boxed{6} \\]",
